{
 "id": "2026-11-18-a",
 "title": "He told doctors to wash their hands. They pushed him out 🧼",
 "description": "In 1847 Ignaz Semmelweis worked in Vienna's maternity clinics. In the doctors' clinic about 1 in 10 mothers died of childbed fever; in the midwives' clinic far fewer. The difference: doctors and students came straight from autopsies. When his colleague Jakob Kolletschka cut himself during a post-mortem and died of a similar illness, Semmelweis ordered hand washing in chlorine solution before touching patients. Deaths fell from about 18% in April 1847 to under 2% by July. Senior doctors resisted, his post wasn't renewed and he left Vienna in 1849. Germ theory (Pasteur) and antiseptic surgery (Lister, 1867) later proved him right.\n\nSources: ScienceAlert (Ignaz Semmelweis); PMC review of Semmelweis (2024).",
 "tags": [
